Yes. A thousand times yes. I wouldn't use svn for the job, though; if
you use one of the various distributed VCS tools instead, you get the
remote synchronization fairly painlessly (you just tell the tool to
push your changes to the remote repo).

I use git (which was designed by Linus Torvalds to use as a change
tracking system for the Linux kernel, and has a number of virtues
including "blindingly fast" and "branches are cheap"), but ISTR it's a
bit awkward to use on Windows so you might want to look at one of the
others - Mercurial, perhaps.

